<h2>How the calculator works</h2>
<p class="lead">Landscape lighting energy use comes down to four numbers: how many fixtures you have, how many watts each one draws, how long they run, and what you pay per kilowatt-hour.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Formula</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>System load</td><td>Fixtures × watts</td><td>20 × 5 W = 100 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy per night</td><td>Load × hours ÷ 1,000</td><td>100 × 6 ÷ 1,000 = 0.60 kWh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy per month</td><td>Nightly kWh × nights</td><td>0.60 × 30 = 18 kWh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly cost</td><td>Monthly kWh × rate</td><td>18 × $0.16 = $2.88</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Getting accurate inputs</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wattage:</strong> check fixture labels or spec sheets. If your system mixes wattages, add them up and divide by the fixture count for an average.</li>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> use your real timer schedule. Sunset-to-11 p.m. averages about five to six hours in Spring; dusk to dawn can exceed 13 hours in December. Our <a href="/tools/sunset-timer-planner/">sunset &amp; timer planner</a> calculates this month by month.</li>
  <li><strong>Rate:</strong> much of the Houston area buys electricity from retail providers, so use the all-in average price per kWh from your Electricity Facts Label or divide your bill total by the kWh used.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Still on halogen?</strong> Press the "Halogen (20 W)" button to see the difference, then run the <a href="/tools/led-savings-calculator/">LED savings calculator</a>.</div>
